\n\nPublished: 2026-07-01\n\nComments: 22 pages, 14 figures, comments are welcome\n\nmath.CO\n\nenumeration planar map poset tamari lattice lattice planar\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe greedy Tamari poset, inspired by the well-studied Tamari lattice, was recently defined by Dermenjian in the more general setting of greedy $ν$-Tamari posets. Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton counted intervals of the greedy $m$-Tamari poset in 2024 by solving a functional equation, and found that they are equi-enumerous to planar $(m+1)$-constellations. In this work, we give a combinatorial proof of this fact for the case $m = 1$, which also gives the refined enumeration conjectured by Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton. This is done by establishing a recursive decomposition of greedy Tamari intervals isomorphic to that of bipartite planar maps. We also propose a more general and refined conjecture for the case of general $m$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposition of Greedy Tamari Intervals and Bipartite Planar Maps (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01206)\nciteturn1academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton counted intervals of the greedy $m$-Tamari poset in 2024 by solving a functional equation, and found that they are equi-enumerous to planar $(m+1)$-constellations. ... We also propose a more general and refined conjecture for the case of general $m$.\nTitle: Decomposition of Greedy Tamari Intervals and Bipartite Planar Maps\nAuthors: Philippe Biane, Wenjie Fang\nDate: Wed Jul  1 17:46:24 2026\n\nThe greedy Tamari poset, inspired by the well-studied Tamari lattice, was recently defined by Dermenjian in the more general setting of greedy $ν$-Tamari posets. Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton counted intervals of the greedy $m$-Tamari poset in 2024 by solving a functional equation, and found that they are equi-enumerous to planar $(m+1)$-constellations. In this work, we give a combinatorial proof of this fact for the case $m = 1$, which also gives the refined enumeration conjectured by Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton. This is done by establishing a recursive decomposition of greedy Tamari intervals isomorphic to that of bipartite planar maps. We also propose a more general and refined conjecture for the case of general $m$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraph Reconstruction - Some New Developments* (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/09728600.2004.12088780)\nciteturn1search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; has proved that the reconstruction number of trees is 3. It has been proved (Bollobas [6], Myrvold ... Class reconstruction number\nF\nG ∉\n \nand each collection of s cards of G will also appear in the deck of some graph of F . Myrvold [43] \nhas proved that the reconstruction number of trees is 3. It has been proved (Bollobas [6], Myrvold \n[41]) that almost every graph has reconstruction number 3. In the case of disconnected graphs, \nMyrvold has proved the following theorems. \n \nTheorem 7.1 [42]  If G is a disconnected graph with at least two different component orders, then \n3\n)\n(\n=\nG\nrn\n. \n \nTheorem 7.2 [42]  If G is a disconnected graph consisting of k components of order c and the \ncomponents are not all isomorphic, then \n3\n)\n(\n=\nG\nrn\n. \n \nTheorem 7.3 [42]  \n2\n)\n(\n+\n≤m\ntG\nrn\n  for \n2\n≥\nt\n  where G  is a connected graph of order m. \n \nThere are graphs with arbitrarily high \n)\n(G\nrn\n  as seen from the following theorem. \n \nTheorem 7.4 [19]   The graph\nn\nK\n2\n has reconstruction number \n2\n  \n  \n2\n≥\n+\nn\nfor\nn\n with \n)1\n( +\nn\n-\nblocking set consisting of the single graph\n1\n1\n−\n+ ∪\nn\nn\nK\nK\n. \n \n    Harary and Plantholt have made the following conjectures. \n \nConjecture [19] :   For any graph G on n vertices,  \n \n2\n2\n)\n(\n+\n≤n\nG\nrn\n \n \nand equality holds if and only if G is \n4\nP  or \nc\nK\n2\n or \nc\n