-----------------------\nThe ally-reconstruction number of a tree with five or more vertices is three (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jgt.3190140203)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # The ally-reconstruction number of a tree with five or more vertices is three\n\nWendy Myrvold, ... Lauri, On the class-reconstruction number of trees.\n\n# The ally-reconstruction number of a tree with five or more vertices is three\n\nWendy Myrvold, \n\nWendy Myrvold\n\nDepartment of Computer Science University of Victoria Victoria, British Columbia, Canada\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nDepartment of Computer Science University of Victoria Victoria, British Columbia, Canada\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nFirst published: June 1990\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.3190140203Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe ally-reconstruction number of a graph G, ally-rn(G), is the minimum number of vertex-deleted subgraphs required in order to identify G up to isomorphism. In this paper, we show that ally-rn(T) = 3 for any tree T with five or more vertices.\n\n\nReferences\n\n  * 1 F. Harary and J. Lauri, On the class-reconstruction number of trees. Oxford Quar. J. Math. (1987 ) to appear.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n\nJune 1990\n\nPages 149-166\n\n### How to cite\n\nMyrvold, W. (1990), The ally-reconstruction number of a tree with five or more vertices is three. J. Graph Theory, 14: 149-166. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.3190140203--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntervals in the greedy Tamari posets (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/381908446_Intervals_in_the_greedy_Tamari_posets)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In [BMC24,Conjecture 6.1], they also conjectured that a certain refined enumeration of greedy m-Tamari intervals is the same as that of planar (m+1)-constellations refined by the profile of certain face degrees. ... ... We prove that Φ induces a bijection between Tamari intervals and minimal realizers.\nIt is thus a surprising news that Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton found and proved in [BMC24] that the number of intervals in the greedy m-Tamari poset, whose definition is inspired by that of the m-Tamari lattice, is equal to the number of certain planar maps. ...\n\n... When defined on m-ballot paths of the same size, we obtain the greedy m-Tamari poset, which was shown in [Der23,Theorem 34] to be isomorphic to the subposet of elements with maximal in-degree in the (m + 1)-Tamari lattice. Soon afterwards, Bousquet-Mélou and Chapoton enumerated in [BMC24] the number of intervals in the greedy m-Tamari poset by solving a functional equation based on a recursive decomposition of such intervals. They found that the counting formula is the same as that of planar (m + 1)-constellations, a family of planar maps first enumerated in [BMS00] using Supported by the project CARPLO (ANR-20-CE40-0007). ...\n\n... bijections. In [BMC24,Conjecture 6.1], they also conjectured that a certain refined enumeration of greedy m-Tamari intervals is the same as that of planar (m+1)-constellations refined by the profile of certain face degrees. ...\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the class reconstruction number of trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17026)\nciteturn1academia20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Harary and Lauri conjectured that the class reconstruction number of trees is 2, that is, each tree has two unlabelled vertex-deleted subtrees that are not both in the deck of any other tree.\nTitle: On the class reconstruction number of trees\nAuthors: Ilia Krasikov, Yehuda Roditty, Bhalchandra D. Thatte\nDate: Thu Dec 28 13:54:28 2023\n\nHarary and Lauri conjectured that the class reconstruction number of trees is 2, that is, each tree has two unlabelled vertex-deleted subtrees that are not both in the deck of any other tree. We show that each tree $T$ can be reconstructed up to isomorphism given two of its unlabelled subgraphs $T-u$ and $T-v$ under the assumption that $u$ and $v$ are chosen in a particular way. Our result does not completely resolve the conjecture of Harary and Lauri since the special property defining $u$ and $v$ cannot be recognised from the given subtrees $T-u$ and $T-v$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGreedy Tamari Intervals & Bipartite Maps (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/2607.01206)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; While a full generalization to $m$m 0-Tamari intervals (and $m$m 1-constellations) is not achieved, the paper puts forth a conjecture—supported by substantial numerical evidence—stating functional equations for the generating functions of greedy $m$m 2-Tamari intervals. ... This paper delivers a combinatorial proof of the enumeration and refined bijection of greedy Tamari intervals and bipartite planar maps for $m$m 9, establishing isomorphic recursive decompositions of intervals and maps and transferring ascent/face statistics bijectively.\n  * The recursive decomposition yields a generating function for intervals, $(m+1)$(m+1)9, identical to that for bipartite maps.\n  * A recursively defined bijection is established between the two classes, transferring refined statistics.\n\n### Theoretical Implications\n\nBy aligning interval operations with the decomposition of planar maps, this work provides a direct combinatorial explanation for the equinumerosity of these objects. The analysis reveals deep structural parallels between tree-based intervals in poset theory and classical map-enumerative techniques, hinting at the universality of the recursive paradigms in algebraic and geometric combinatorics.\n\n## Generalization and Conjectural Extensions\n\nWhile a full generalization to $m$m 0-Tamari intervals (and $m$m 1-constellations) is not achieved, the paper puts forth a conjecture—supported by substantial numerical evidence—stating functional equations for the generating functions of greedy $m$m 2-Tamari intervals. For $m$m 3, this involves higher arity analogues of the tree and map structures (e.g., $m$m 4-ballot paths, $m$m 5-branch trees, $m$m 6-constellations), and suggests the existence of recursive decompositions analogous to those constructed for $m$m 7.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis paper delivers a combinatorial proof of the enumeration and refined bijection of greedy Tamari intervals and bipartite planar maps for $m$m 9, establishing isomorphic recursive decompositions of intervals and maps and transferring ascent/face statistics bijectively. The work also formulates conjectural extensions to higher arity via $(m+1)$(m+1)0-branch trees and $(m+1)$(m+1)1-constellations, stimulating future research into the combinatorial structures underlying map enumeration and Tamari-type posets.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntervals in the greedy Tamari posets (https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.18077)\nciteturn1academia21 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.6 years ago; Title: Intervals in the greedy Tamari posets ... We find again a simple formula, which also counts certain planar maps (of prescribed size) called $(m+1)$-constellations.\nTitle: Intervals in the greedy Tamari posets\nAuthors: Mireille Bousquet-Mélou, Frédéric Chapoton\nDate: Fri Mar 31 14:14:13 2023\n\nWe consider a greedy version of the $m$-Tamari order defined on $m$-Dyck paths, recently introduced by Dermenjian. Inspired by intriguing connections between intervals in the ordinary 1-Tamari order and planar triangulations, and more generally by the existence of simple formulas counting intervals in the ordinary $m$-Tamari orders, we investigate the number of intervals in the greedy order on $m$-Dyck paths of fixed size. We find again a simple formula, which also counts certain planar maps (of prescribed size) called $(m+1)$-constellations. For instance, when $m=1$ the number of intervals in the greedy order on 1-Dyck paths of length $2n$ is proved to be $\\frac{3\\cdot 2^{n-1}}{(n+1)(n+2)} \\binom{2n}{n}$, which is also the number of bipartite maps with $n$ edges. Our approach is recursive, and uses a ``catalytic'' parameter, namely the length of the final descent of the upper path of the interval. The resulting bivariate generating function is algebraic for all $m$. We show that the same approach can be used to count intervals in the ordinary $m$-Tamari lattices as well. We thus recover the earlier result of the first author, Fusy and Pr\\'eville-Ratelle, who were using a different catalytic parameter.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA survey of some open questions in reconstruction numbers (https://www.academia.edu/23384925/A_survey_of_some_open_questions_in_reconstruction_numbers?force_claim_to_highlight=true)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Actually, Harary and Lauri [12] had earlier proved the weaker result that if C is the class of trees then, for any tree T , the class ally reconstruction number Crn(T ) is at most 3—but in their paper, in most junctures of the proof, Crn(T ) was shown to be 2, and they made the following conjecture. ... 3.1 Tree / unicyclic graph pair In order to tackle this difficult problem Myrvold concentrated on the ad- versary reconstruction number of trees, particularly the largest possible number of cards in common between a tree and a non-tree.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nON THE CLASS-RECONSTRUCTION NUMBER OF TREES (https://academic.oup.com/qjmath/article-abstract/39/1/47/1607946)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 38.7 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; # ON THE CLASS-RECONSTRUCTION NUMBER OF TREES\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVertex-deleted and E